==============================================
1) Which OOME types are sometimes recoverable?
==============================================
✅ A) “Heap space” OOME (sometimes recoverable)
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space

When it can recover
 > The OOME happens in a request path (e.g., building a big response)
 > You catch it (or your framework catches it), return 503/500
 > Traffic drops (or you trip a circuit breaker / rate limit)
 >  GC runs and frees enough for the process to continue
        Traffic spike / big allocations => heap hits Xmx => OOME thrown in request => Request fails fast (503) => Load reduces -> GC catches up -> app continues

When it won’t recover
 > You have a real leak (live set keeps growing)
 > Even after load drops, memory doesn’t go down
 > You keep hitting OOME repeatedly until the process is useless

✅ B) “unable to create new native thread” (sometimes recoverable)
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: unable to create new native thread
Cause
 >Too many threads, or not enough native memory for stacks

How it can recover
 > Some threads exit
 > Thread pools stop growing (bounded pools)
 > Load drops: But usually this indicates a design/ops issue (unbounded thread creation).

⚠️ C) “Metaspace” OOME (rarely recoverable)
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace
Usually due to:
 > dynamic classloading leaks (common with hot-reload tooling, bytecode generation, proxy factories)
 > mis-sized metaspace
 > Metaspace is freed only when classloaders are GC’d. If it’s a leak, it won’t recover.

❌ D) “GC overhead limit exceeded” (typically not recoverable)
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: GC overhead limit exceeded
 This means:
 >JVM is spending most time in GC
 > reclaiming very little

the system is in a death spiral

It might limp, but operationally it’s “restart me.”

2) What “recovery” actually looks like in a lending API
Scenario: request creates a big object graph

Example:

a request triggers a fan-out

aggregates large DTOs

accidentally loads too much (e.g., huge list / big PDF / large JSON)

If you handle it well:

the request fails

memory is freed after request scope ends

service returns to normal

ASCII:

Request builds 200MB response object
       |
       v
OOME thrown
       |
       v
Request aborted -> references dropped
       |
       v
GC frees them -> app continues

3) How to make OOME “recoverable” (real techniques)
A) Fail fast and shed load

Rate limit

Circuit breakers (stop calling downstream)

Return 503 quickly instead of piling more allocations

This prevents “keep allocating into the fire.”

B) Bound memory-heavy things

Limit max payload size

Limit max list sizes / pagination defaults

Bounded thread pools / bounded queues

Cap caches (Caffeine with max size/weight)

C) Keep heap headroom

If you run heap at 95% constantly, any spike tips you into OOME.
Keep steady-state heap at ~70–80% of Xmx.

D) Restart policy

Even if it “can recover”, many orgs choose:

detect OOME

kill the process intentionally

let OpenShift restart it clean

Why? Because after an OOME, the heap may be fragmented, performance degraded, and you risk repeated failures.

Common practice:

-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError


(or legacy: -XX:+CrashOnOutOfMemoryError)

This turns OOME into a controlled restart instead of limping.

4) OOMKilled is NOT recoverable

Just to contrast:

OOMKilled (cgroup) -> SIGKILL -> process dies immediately


No recovery path. It restarts.

ASCII:

Total RSS > limit -> KILL -> restart

5) How it manifests when it “recovers”

You’ll see:

a burst of 500/503 errors

latency spike

then it stabilizes

In logs:

OutOfMemoryError ... (one-off)


but the pod stays Running and doesn’t restart.

In Grafana:

heap_used hits Xmx -> errors spike -> heap drops -> back to baseline


ASCII:

Heap Used
|          ██████████  <- hits Xmx
|        ████████████
|   ██████
| ███
+-----------------------> time
      errors spike

Rule of thumb (bank microservice ops)

If you see one OOME under exceptional payload/traffic and then normal: “recoverable incident”

If you see repeated OOMEs: treat as leak / bad sizing → restart + fix root cause

